      "text": "Regardless, EO was highly effective in oxidizing 14C-PFOA in the foamate from both sources with half-lives (T 1/2) ranging from 8.7 to 15 min. While water chemistry differences between source waters may have influenced foam fractionation and require additional investigations, tank experiments provide the first proof-of-concept experiment using 14C-PFASs that foam fractionation and electrochemical oxidation can be used in tandem to treat PFAS-contaminated water. Keywords: PFAS enrichment; PFAS removal; wastewater treatment plant (WWTP); cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) 1. Introduction Per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances (PFASs) are a family of manmade chemicals that have been heavily used in manufacturing, often without adequate disposal. Commonly Environments 2025, 12, 185 https://doi.org/10.3390/environments12060185 Environments 2025, 12, 185 2 of 13 used to improve the quality of commercial products by making them resistant to heat, oil, stains, and grease, PFASs are found in many household goods and industrial products like surfactants, emulsifiers, and fire-fighting foams. Unfortunately, the chemical properties that make PFASs useful from a commercial perspective also make them mobile and recalcitrant once released into the environment [1]. As true xenobiotics, PFASs have no known natural decomposition processes, and the"
